## 🏗️ Application Architecture

The application consists of two primary services:

### Frontend

The frontend is exposed through Kubernetes and communicates with the backend through the Kubernetes Service DNS name.

```text
Frontend
   │
   ▼
frontend-service
   │
   ▼
Backend API
```

### Backend

The backend is a Flask application exposing health functionality through:

```text
/api/health
```

Ingress rewrites the `/api` prefix before forwarding the request to the backend.

For example:

```text
https://devops.local/api/health
```

is routed through Ingress to:

```text
backend-service:5000/health
```

The backend returns:

```json
{
  "service": "devops-cloud-platform",
  "status": "healthy"
}
```

---

## 🐳 Docker

Both application components are containerized.

### Backend image

```text
kunalsingh9038/devops-cloud-backend:<git-sha>
```

### Frontend image

```text
kunalsingh9038/devops-cloud-frontend:<git-sha>
```

Instead of relying only on `latest`, the pipeline uses the Git commit SHA as the image version.

Example:

```text
kunalsingh9038/devops-cloud-backend:94a6f2d
kunalsingh9038/devops-cloud-frontend:94a6f2d
```

This makes deployments traceable to an exact source-code revision.

---

## 🔄 CI/CD Pipeline

Jenkins performs the following stages:

### 1. Checkout

Jenkins checks out the source code from GitHub.

### 2. Set Image Tag

The short Git commit SHA is extracted:

```bash
git rev-parse --short HEAD
```

Example:

```text
94a6f2d
```

### 3. Backend Tests

The backend tests run automatically using Pytest.

```text
2 passed
```

### 4. Build Docker Images

Jenkins builds:

```text
devops-cloud-backend:<SHA>
devops-cloud-frontend:<SHA>
```

### 5. Push to Docker Hub

Jenkins authenticates with Docker Hub using Jenkins credentials and pushes both images.

### 6. Deploy to Kubernetes

Jenkins connects to the Windows Minikube agent and updates the Kubernetes deployments:

```bash
kubectl set image deployment/backend ...
kubectl set image deployment/frontend ...
```

### 7. Rollout Verification

Jenkins waits for Kubernetes to complete the rollout:

```bash
kubectl rollout status deployment/backend
kubectl rollout status deployment/frontend
```

The pipeline only reports success when both deployments successfully roll out.

---

## ☸️ Kubernetes Architecture

The application runs on a Minikube Kubernetes cluster.

### Deployments

```text
backend
├── backend replica 1
└── backend replica 2

frontend
├── frontend replica 1
└── frontend replica 2
```

Current deployment configuration:

```text
Backend:  2 replicas
Frontend: 2 replicas
```

### Services

The cluster uses Kubernetes Services for internal communication:

```text
frontend-service
backend-service
```

The frontend communicates with the backend using Kubernetes DNS rather than hard-coded pod IP addresses.

---

## ❤️ Health Checks

Kubernetes uses readiness and liveness probes to monitor application health.

### Readiness Probe

Determines whether a pod is ready to receive traffic.

### Liveness Probe

Determines whether the application is still healthy.

This allows Kubernetes to avoid routing traffic to unhealthy pods and restart containers when necessary.

---

## 🌐 Ingress

The project uses the NGINX Ingress Controller.

Traffic is routed using the hostname:

```text
devops.local
```

### Routing

```text
https://devops.local/
        │
        ▼
frontend-service

https://devops.local/api/health
        │
        ▼
backend-service
```

The `/api` prefix is rewritten before the request reaches Flask.

---

## 🔐 HTTPS / TLS

HTTPS is configured using a Kubernetes TLS Secret.

```text
devops-cloud-tls
```

The development environment uses a self-signed certificate for:

```text
devops.local
```

Example request:

```bash
curl.exe -k -H "Host: devops.local" https://127.0.0.1:<PORT>/api/health
```

> The TLS private key and certificate files are intentionally excluded from Git.

---

## 📊 Monitoring

The project includes:

### Prometheus

Prometheus collects Kubernetes and application-related metrics.

### Grafana

Grafana is used to visualize the collected metrics.

The monitoring stack is deployed into:

```text
monitoring
```

namespace.

Prometheus is configured as a Grafana data source and has been successfully queried from Grafana.

