## ☸️ Kubernetes Commands

Check cluster:

```bash
kubectl get nodes
```

Check deployments:

```bash
kubectl get deployments
```

Check pods:

```bash
kubectl get pods -o wide
```

Check services:

```bash
kubectl get services
```

Check ingress:

```bash
kubectl get ingress
```

Check deployment images:

```bash
kubectl get deployment backend -o jsonpath="{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}"
```

```bash
kubectl get deployment frontend -o jsonpath="{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}"
```

Check rollout:

```bash
kubectl rollout status deployment/backend
```

```bash
kubectl rollout status deployment/frontend
```

---

## 🔎 Verify the Current Deployment

The CI/CD pipeline automatically deploys the Git SHA generated by Jenkins.

For example:

```text
Backend:
kunalsingh9038/devops-cloud-backend:94a6f2d

Frontend:
kunalsingh9038/devops-cloud-frontend:94a6f2d
```

This provides a direct relationship between:

```text
Git Commit
     ↓
Docker Image
     ↓
Kubernetes Deployment
```

---

## 🔁 Deployment Strategy

The Kubernetes deployments use multiple replicas and rolling updates.

When Jenkins deploys a new image:

```text
Old Version
   │
   ├── Pod 1
   └── Pod 2
          ↓
New Version
   │
   ├── Pod 1
   └── Pod 2
```

Kubernetes manages the rollout while Jenkins waits for successful completion.

This provides a safer deployment process than manually replacing containers.

